Original commit body from 49a0b764ca36fcbb7400cf0cd0b81850a5572299:

`--backend rust-analyzer` runs `rust-analyzer scip <workspace>`, which
EXECUTES the analysed project's build.rs and proc macros; `--backend
scip-python` runs `scip-python index`. Both are reachable from runtime
cmd_* handlers, and both called subprocess.run through an injectable
local (`run = runner or subprocess.run`), so no edge reached a catalogued
launch, and runtime-cli-no-subprocess said runtime subcommands shell out
only through repo_inspection. Reproduced in the 2026-10-25 premise check
with fake binaries on PATH: a fixture's own .hypergumbo.toml switched the
scip-python launch on, and HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER=1 alone (no trust
grant) launched `rust-analyzer scip`.

- safety_zones gains backend_index_scip_python and
  backend_index_rust_analyzer (zone analyser_backend), one per backend so a
  claim can permit the static indexer and prohibit the one that runs code.
  Their own zone, not repo_inspection: that zone's recorded split trigger
  (owner, 2026-08-11) is anything that reads repository content and acts on
  it, which indexing does.
- Both invoke modules default to the wrapper; `runner` stays the test seam.
- The scip-python scratch dir comes from safety_zones.tmp_artifact_dir.
- docs/hypergumbo-self-catalog/analyser_backend_sinks.yaml declares the
  zone, loaded from the claims file.
- runtime-cli-no-subprocess now names both launches and what enables each
  (flag, env var, or trust grant / config tier, any one suffices -- the
  item's "after a trust-backend grant" was wrong), and the scip-python
  --version probe; SECURITY.md regenerated; the three INV-tusos findings
  leave the I/O allowlist and the two wrapper sites enter it.

